<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav signed_in?={@signed_in?} current={:docs} />
  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="investigate-a-service"
      updated="October 28, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"prepare", "1. Start a test container"},
        {"policy", "2. Check the approval rule"},
        {"investigate", "3. Ask the agent to investigate"},
        {"request", "4. Request a restart"},
        {"approve", "5. Review the approval"},
        {"verify", "6. Verify and clean up"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="investigate-a-service">
        <:dek>
          Run a throwaway nginx container on a dev host, then let your agent inspect it and
          request a restart. You approve the restart in emisar and check the result.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          A Linux dev host or VM with Docker and a connected runner. Use a machine you can change
          freely, not production.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          The
          <.link navigate={~p"/packs/docker"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">docker</.link>
          pack. The installer offers it as a starter pack when it finds Docker; otherwise run <code>sudo emisar pack install docker</code>.
          The runner restart in step 1 loads it.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          An MCP client that has completed the <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >quickstart</.link>.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          Access to approvals in the console. A policy change needs an owner or admin.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="prepare">1. Start a test container</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Start nginx in a container and check that it answers:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">docker run -d --name emisar-demo -p 8080:80 nginx:alpine
curl -sI http://localhost:8080 | head -1</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        You should see <code>HTTP/1.1 200 OK</code>. The runner also needs access to the Docker
        daemon. Add its user to the <code>docker</code> group and restart the runner, as the
        docker pack's setup describes:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">sudo usermod -aG docker emisar
sudo systemctl restart emisar</.docs_code>
      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="Docker access is root access">
        Anything in the <code>docker</code> group can control the host through Docker. Do this on
        a dev machine, and remove the access when you finish (step 6).
      </.docs_callout>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open the host in
        <a href={~p"/app/runners"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">Runners</a>
        and confirm that <code>docker.ps</code>, <code>docker.logs</code>, and
        <code>docker.restart</code>
        are available. The docker pack has 28 actions: 16 low risk, 3 medium, 8 high, and 1
        critical.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="policy">2. Check the approval rule</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        In <a href={~p"/app/policies"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">Policies</a>,
        inspect the policy that applies to this runner. With the shipped defaults,
        <code>docker.ps</code>
        (low risk) and <code>docker.logs</code>
        (medium) run on their own, and <code>docker.restart</code>
        (high) waits for approval.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Check that no action override or standing grant (a reusable approval) lets the restart
        run on its own. See <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >policies and approvals</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="investigate">3. Ask the agent to investigate</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Use the runner name shown in the console. For example:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Prompt · replace dev-box with your runner name">Use emisar to check the emisar-demo container on runner dev-box.
List the containers, then read its recent logs. Summarize what you find.
Do not restart it yet.</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The agent finds <code>docker.ps</code>
        and <code>docker.logs</code>
        and runs them without asking you. This is what came back in our test; the nginx log
        includes the <code>curl</code>
        request from step 1:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="docker.ps and docker.logs results">docker.ps · exit 0
CONTAINER ID   IMAGE          COMMAND                  CREATED          STATUS          PORTS                                     NAMES
12d88303806d   nginx:alpine   "/docker-entrypoint.…"   58 seconds ago   Up 58 seconds   0.0.0.0:8080->80/tcp, [::]:8080->80/tcp   emisar-demo

docker.logs · exit 0
/docker-entrypoint.sh: Configuration complete; ready for start up
192.168.215.1 - - [29/Oct/2026:04:00:28 +0000] "HEAD / HTTP/1.1" 200 0 "-" "curl/8.7.1" "-"</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        In <a
          href={~p"/app/runs"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Runs</a>, check the
        target, actions, arguments, and output of each call. If a call is denied or fails, fix
        the problem it shows before you continue.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="request">4. Request a restart</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Prompt · use the same runner">Restart the emisar-demo container on dev-box through emisar.
Reason: try the approval flow on a test container.
Wait for the result. Do not send another restart while this one is pending.</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        emisar answers the agent with <code>pending_approval</code>
        and a link to the approval. Open <a
          href={~p"/app/approvals"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Approvals</a>.
        The restart waits there, and the container is still running. If it ran right away,
        check the policy and grants before you try again.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="approve">5. Review the approval</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The approval shows the risk (high), your runner, and the exact command: <code>docker restart -t 10 emisar-demo</code>. Below it are the agent's reason and why
        the request stopped: "The account policy requires approval for high-risk actions by
        default." Leave "Allow the agent to reuse this approval" off, then approve this one
        run. If anything looks wrong, deny it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        By default you can approve your own request. If your team requires another approver or
        several votes, follow that rule. After you approve, let the agent wait for the result of
        the original request instead of sending a new one.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="verify">6. Verify and clean up</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open the run and wait for its result. Exit code <code>0</code>
        means <code>docker restart</code>
        finished, and its output is just the container name, <code>emisar-demo</code>. Ask the
        agent to list the containers again. In our test, the STATUS column then read
        <code>Up 16 seconds</code>
        while CREATED still said about a minute ago. Then check nginx yourself:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">curl -sI http://localhost:8080 | head -1</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        You should see <code>HTTP/1.1 200 OK</code> again.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="If the result is missing">
        Inspect the host before repeating a restart. A connection failure can hide a result
        even when the action ran. Use
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/runs"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">run history</.link>
        and the container's state to decide what to do next.
      </.docs_callout>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open <a href={~p"/app/audit"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">Audit</a>.
        Newest first, it lists the second <code>docker.ps</code>, then "Run succeeded" for <code>docker.restart</code>, "Approval granted", "Approval decision recorded", and "Run
        awaiting approval", followed by the earlier reads. When you are done, remove the
        container and the runner's Docker access:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">docker rm -f emisar-demo
sudo gpasswd -d emisar docker
sudo systemctl restart emisar</.docs_code>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>
  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
